    "message": {
     "content": "### The Short Answer\n\n**This is an exceptional, top-of-band (90th+ percentile) offer for an L6 Product Manager at Google.** \n\nYou should **not walk away**, and you do not need to push hard. Because Google is your first choice and you have no competing offers, your best strategy is either to **accept directly** or make **one low-risk, polite final ask** before signing.\n\n---\n\n### Compensation Breakdown & Comparison\n\n#### 1. Current Meta IC6 Compensation\n* **Base:** $240,000\n* **Bonus (15%):** $36,000\n* **RSUs (Annual Run-Rate):** ~$250,000 (~$200,000 unvested over next 12 months)\n* **Total Annual Comp:** **~$526,000** (or ~$476,000 over the next 12 months)\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Google L6 Offer\n* **Base Salary:** $260,000 *(near the top of the posted $183k\u2013$271k range)*\n* **Target Annual Bonus (20%):** $52,000\n* **Sign-on Bonus:** $200,000 *(fully offsets the $200k of Meta RSUs you would forfeit over the next year)*\n* **Equity (GSUs):** $1,340,000 over 4 years\n\n#### How Google Equity Vests\nGoogle standardly frontloads initial equity grants rather than using an even 25%/year split. A typical frontloaded schedule is **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** (or **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%**):\n\n| Metric | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 | Year 4 | 4-Year Average |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $260,000 | $260,000 | $260,000 | $260,000 | $260,000 |\n| **Bonus (Target)** | $52,000 | $52,000 | $52,000 | $52,000 | $52,000 |\n| **GSU Vesting (33/33/22/12)** | $442,200 | $442,200 | $294,800 | $160,800 | $335,000 |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $200,000 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 | $50,000 |\n| **Total Compensation (TC)** | **~$954,200** | **~$754,200** | **~$606,800** | **~$472,800** | **~$697,000/yr** |\n\n*(If using the 38/32/20/10 schedule, Year 1 TC crosses **$1,020,000**.)*\n\n#### Key Takeaways:\n1. **Year 1:** You are nearly doubling your current compensation (jumping from ~$526k to **~$954k+**).\n2. **4-Year Average:** At **~$697k/year** (or **~$647k/year** without sign-on), this is a **25%\u201332% bump** over your Meta baseline.\n3. **The Year 3/4 \"Cliff\":** Because Google frontloads equity, Years 3 and 4 drop off. However, Google grants **annual equity refreshers** based on performance. Starting in Year 2, those annual refreshers stack on top of your initial grant to backfill the drop in Years 3 and 4.\n\n---\n\n### How This Compares to Market\n\n* **Base ($260,000):** Sits at the **96th percentile** of the posted salary range ($183k\u2013$271k).\n* **Sign-on ($200,000):** Well above standard L6 sign-ons (which typically range from $50k to $100k). Google used this to neutralize your unvested Meta equity completely.\n* **Equity ($1.34M):** Standard L6 initial GSU grants typically land between $800,000 and $1,100,000. At $1.34M ($335k/year average), Google has already calibrated you at the top end of the L6 band.\n\n---\n\n### Recommendation: Should You Accept or Negotiate?\n\n* **Walk Away?** **No.** It is a massive step up financially, fits your career goals, and is your #1 choice.\n* **Aggressive Negotiation?** **No.** Since you do not have competing offers, pushing too hard or demanding another $100k+ risks creating friction with the hiring team.\n* **One-Shot \"Closing\" Ask?** **Yes, you can make a single, low-risk ask.** In Big Tech, making one polite closing ask will not get an offer rescinded, and recruiters often keep a small buffer ($10k\u2013$20k base or $50k equity) to guarantee a close.\n\n---\n\n### What to Ask For & How to Frame It\n\nIf you choose to negotiate, use the **\"One-Shot Closing\" framework**: state that Google is your absolute top choice and give them a specific, reasonable target that guarantees you will sign immediately.\n\n#### The Target Request:\n* **Equity:** Ask for an increase from **$1.34M to $1.45M\u2013$1.50M** (a ~$25k\u2013$40k/yr bump). Equity has the most flexibility in Google's hiring budgets.\n* *Alternative (Base):* Ask for base salary to be rounded up to the top of the posted band (**$270,000**).\n\n#### Sample Script for the Recruiter:\n\n> *\"Thank you so much for putting together such a strong offer. I'm genuinely excited about the team, the scope of the role, and Google is unequivocally my #1 choice.*\n>\n> *I want to be completely transparent: because leaving Meta means stepping away from ongoing equity growth and an established track record, I\u2019d love to get this to a number where I can sign immediately today.*\n> \n> *If we can bridge the equity to **$1.45M over 4 years** (or adjust base to **$270k**), I am ready to sign the offer letter today without hesitation.\"*\n\n#### If they say Yes:\nSign immediately.\n\n#### If they say No:\nRecruiters will typically say, *\"We put our best foot forward with this package and can't move further.\"* Simply reply:\n\n> *\"I completely understand and appreciate you checking with the compensation committee. Google is my top choice and I love the team, so I am thrilled to accept the offer as written.\"*",
